From 71a12d8f109a8a5aa1601d491045c3d435b65fb9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Woodhouse <dwmw@amazon.co.uk>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 21:45:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] network: add tcp_local mode, and prefer IPv6 for sync
 connections

Order the addresses which umdns advertises for a peer in the usual
getaddrinfo() style - link-local IPv6 first, then global IPv6, then
Legacy IP - and connect to the most preferred, falling back down the
list when a connection fails. Link-local is renumbering-proof, and a
peer which cannot accept it (an older dawn only listens on Legacy IP)
is still reached through the fallback.

With the new option tcp_local set, additionally reject inbound sync
connections from any address which is neither link-local nor a unicast
address within a prefix directly attached to a local interface. The
sync protocol trusts whoever can connect: without use_symm_enc the
message content is unauthenticated, and even with it, the framing is
parsed pre-auth. An off-link attacker can spoof an on-link source
address but can never complete a TCP handshake with one, since the
return path leads back to the local link.

Peers discovered via umdns are on-link by definition, so with
network_option 2 the restriction costs nothing, and old and new dawn
instances interoperate in both directions. Deployments with a routed
server_ip hub must leave tcp_local unset on the hub, as it cannot
enumerate its spokes.

The umdns browse results carry multiple "ipv6" members for a host with
more than one address, which a one-shot blobmsg_parse() policy cannot
see; walk the attributes by hand.

Signed-off-by: David Woodhouse <dwmw@amazon.co.uk>
